Seborrhoeic Dermatitis (infant)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Greasy yellow scales, Erythematous plaques, Seborrheic distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe weeping and bleeding from the scalp and behind the ears, causing extreme distress and sleep disruption in an infant.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (Infant), Parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 3-month-old infant with a rapidly worsening, thick crusting rash on the scalp and face that had suddenly begun weeping clear fluid and bleeding when rubbed. The infant was highly irritable, unable to sleep, and the parents were in acute distress fearing a severe systemic infection. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute staphylococcal impetiginization or serious generalized dermatitis, and to initiate immediate, safe topical therapy to break the itch cycle before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "His scalp is covered in these thick, yellow crusts that smell awful, and now behind his ears is bright red, weeping, and bleeding because he keeps rubbing it on the sheets."
History of Present Illness: The infant developed a mild cradle cap presentation 4 weeks ago with thick crusts on the scalp. Over the past 48 hours, the rash has spread significantly, presenting as poorly marginated erythematous plaques with greasy yellow scales extending down the forehead, into the eyebrows, and severely affecting the post-auricular folds. The parents report the infant is experiencing moderate to severe pruritus, constantly rubbing their head against the mattress, which has led to superficial excoriation, serous weeping, and pinpoint bleeding behind the ears. The infant is extremely irritable, crying continuously, and feeding poorly due to discomfort. No history of fever or vomiting.
Functional Status: Infant, entirely dependent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe parental anxiety; risk of secondary bacterial infection in the weeping post-auricular folds.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Not indicated | HR: 145 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 35 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 5.8 Kg
General: Irritable infant, crying vigorously, constantly rubbing the sides of the head against the mother's chest.
Physical Exam: The vertex of the scalp is covered in a thick, confluent, waxy, yellow-brown crust (classic cradle cap). The forehead, bilateral eyebrows, and nasolabial folds display poorly marginated erythematous plaques with fine, greasy yellow scales. The bilateral post-auricular folds are intensely erythematous, macerated, and weeping serous fluid, with a small 2mm linear fissure on the right side causing minor bleeding. Despite the weeping, there is no thick, honey-coloured crusting (impetigo), no spreading, hot, indurated cellulitic edge, and no fluctuance. The infant is afebrile and well-perfused.
Environmental Observations: Parents have applied multiple thick, occlusive natural oils (e.g., olive oil) which have exacerbated the yeast overgrowth.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Infantile Seborrheic Dermatitis (Cradle Cap) with acute intertriginous weeping - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Atopic Dermatitis (Infantile Eczema) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly greasy, yellow nature of the scales, the specific involvement of the axillae/post-auricular areas early on, and the relative sparing of the extensor cheeks and limbs which are classic for atopic eczema.

Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The primary waxy, greasy scaling over the scalp and face; the weeping behind the ears is serous and intertriginous rather than the classic friable, honey-coloured crusts of a primary staphylococcal infection.

Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of petechiae, purpuric lesions, or systemic signs of illness; presentation aligns perfectly with severe, simple seborrheic dermatitis exacerbated by occlusive oils.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The infant presents with a severe, highly symptomatic exacerbation of infantile seborrheic dermatitis, complicated by maceration and weeping in the flexures due to excessive friction and parental application of occlusive oils. Triage accurately flagged the weeping, bleeding, and inconsolable irritability in an infant as an urgent scenario requiring assessment to rule out a severe secondary bacterial infection or systemic illness. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation confirmed the benign, albeit severe, inflammatory nature of the condition, explicitly ruled out impetigo, and allowed for the immediate cessation of the harmful home remedies. Prescribing a very mild topical steroid and antifungal quickly suppressed the acute inflammation and safely managed the severe parental panic.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the parents to immediately cease applying olive/coconut oil to the scalp, as this feeds the Malassezia yeast. Prescribed a mild combination cream (Hydrocortisone 1% / Clotrimazole 1%) to be applied strictly to the weeping post-auricular folds and red facial plaques BD for a maximum of 5 days. Recommended gentle mechanical removal of scalp crusts using a soft baby brush during a bath with a mild baby shampoo.
Positive Behavioral Change: By entirely stopping the use of thick cooking oils on the baby's head, you will starve the yeast that is causing this severe reaction, allowing the skin to finally dry out and heal. By applying the prescribed medicated cream precisely twice a day to the weeping red areas behind the ears, you will rapidly shut off the severe itch and inflammation, allowing the baby to finally sleep comfortably tonight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP/Pediatrician review in 7 days to ensure the weeping has resolved and discuss long-term gentle skin care.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the red areas begin draining thick yellow pus, if the redness spreads rapidly across the neck or chest, or if the infant develops a fever over 38.0C or stops feeding, present immediately to the Pediatric Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Infantile seborrheic dermatitis is extremely common and harmless, though it can look very severe when it flares.
Clinical Course: The weeping behind the ears and the intense redness will improve dramatically within 48 hours of using the medicated cream. The thick scalp crusts will take several weeks to slowly loosen and fall off.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the skin behind the ears carefully. Do not use the steroid cream for longer than 5 days to prevent skin thinning.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A harmless yeast that lives on all babies' skin has overgrown because of the natural oils produced by the baby, made worse by the extra oils you applied. The combination cream uses a mild steroid to instantly stop the red, itchy allergic reaction, and an anti-fungal to clear away the yeast.
Actionable Steps: Wash the baby's hair normally with baby shampoo. Do not forcefully pick or scrape the thick yellow crusts off the scalp, as this will cause bleeding and infection; let them fall off naturally.

SMS INFORMATION

Assessed for severe infantile cradle cap and weeping rash. Diagnosed Infantile Seborrheic Dermatitis. Commenced mild steroid/antifungal cream. Cease using oils on scalp. GP review 7 days.
SMS EDUCATION

Stop applying olive/coconut oil to the scalp. Use the prescribed cream thinly twice daily for 5 days max. Seek ED care if the baby develops a fever or stops feeding.